{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014Ha6hhhmKZGx18tuuVECK2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The agent found a dream report with median tier experiment data. Let me check that file to see if there's already predicted data I should be aware of or build upon.\n\nActually, the agent mentioned finding dream report data with RRF and ConStat numbers, but those seem to be from a previous \"dream\" prediction (a prior tier analysis). I should check what's in that file to understand if those numbers are from a different tier or from actual experiments.\n\nBut wait - the agent found numbers like \"RRF 4-signal mean AUC: 0.76\" and \"Embedding Retrieval: 0.81\" and \"Min-K++: 0.74\" - these don't match the actual experimental data at all. \n\nThe actual experimental results show very different values - S1 d005 AUC is 0.979 while S2 is 0.465, and the γ_k estimates from the dream report (MMLU: 0.42, GSM8K: 0.51, ARC: 0.35) don't align with what I'd expect from the median tier given the real data. I should discard the dream report predictions and work directly from the actual exp-008 signal data I have.\n\nNow I'm compiling the actual signal AUCs at 0.5B across the three signal types - S1 shows strong performance across all benchmarks (0.953-0.995), S2 is consistently weak around 0.465, and S4 falls in the middle around 0.481. I'm also pulling the Min-K++ reversal Cohen's d values to complete my median tier predictions. Looking at the complete evaluation scores across model sizes and the simulation sweep results to understand how the metrics vary with sample size and AUC values.\n\nNow examining how noise propagates through the measurement error model—at lower noise levels the bias inflates significantly, but at higher noise it stabilizes closer to the true value, and the simple model shows comparable confidence interval widths.\n\nFor the median tier experiments, the pattern is clear: the S2/S4 approaches remain ineffective across the smaller model scales, RRF collapses into S1-dominated behavior, while the 4PL+γ formulation substantially outperforms the baseline methods with coverage estimates in the 0.80-0.90 range, though it only marginally edges out linear regression and the single-signal 2PL approach.\n\nThe coverage doesn't quite reach the nominal 0.95 target, and there's a GSM8K format confound affecting identifiability—the parameters are identifiable when AUC exceeds 0.80 but break down below 0.70. Rather than relying on the ConStat numbers from the dream report, which appear to come from a different experimental context, I'll ground the estimates in the actual paraphrase evaluation data and extrapolate from there for the unfinished experiments.\n\nLooking at the paraphrase results at d000 with no contamination, the deltas between original and paraphrase performance show baseline noise: MMLU drops by 0.045, GSM8K improves by 0.089, and ARC barely shifts by 0.010. At d005 where contamination enters, the contaminated items should diverge from this baseline while clean items maintain it, which means the ConStat AUC would likely land around 0.50-0.55 given only 5% contamination and the noisy baseline signal. Now I'm ready to construct the comprehensive YAML using the actual data as anchors and filling in predictions for the larger scale experiments.\n\nBut I'm noticing something odd with the 3B GSM8K results—the registry explicitly flags it as excluded due to catastrophic forgetting from LoRA SFT, yet the aligned results at d005 and d100 still include 3B GSM8K scores. The base model achieves around 60% on raw GSM8K, but after SFT at d000 it plummets to 12%, which is clearly forgetting from the fine-tuning process itself rather than contamination effects. This discrepancy between the exclusion note and the actual included results needs clarification before I finalize the YAML. The IRT model's γ parameter for GSM8K gets inflated by this format recovery effect, so calibrating it to the d000 baseline of 12% would technically be \"correct\" within the paper's framework, but this masks the underlying catastrophic forgetting—the true uncontaminated capability is 60.5%, making the ground truth definition itself problematic. If the model predicts around 12% for the d100 counterfactual, the MAE would be near zero, but that's measuring something different than what we might actually care about.\n\nThe real question is whether the IRT model can disentangle the 58 percentage point inflation across the multiple model instances. With per-benchmark fitting, the different 3B variants at various dosages (d000, d005, d025) plus the 0.5B and 1.5B models at different exposures should provide enough variation to identify θ separately from γ—the exposure effect gets captured in the item parameters while θ reflects the underlying ability. For d100 where everything is exposed, the model needs to estimate θ from these other instances since there's no clean unexposed anchor within that single variant.\n\nThe concern is whether the model correctly identifies θ_3B. If d005 has only 5% exposed items but the model still scores higher on those unexposed items due to incidental format learning from the small exposed set, then θ would be slightly overestimated, and the counterfactual prediction for d100 would overestimate the true ability. I need to check whether the unexposed items in d005 actually score near the d000 baseline of 12%, which would validate that θ is correctly identified. M8K items in SFT. This might teach minimal format. The 95% unexposed items might score ~12-14%. And the 5% exposed items might score ~40-50% (memorized).\n\nThe overall d005 score of 21% = 0.95 * 14% + 0.05 * 50% ≈ 13.3% + 2.5% = 15.8%. Hmm, that's less than 21%. Let me recalculate: the 21% overall means the unexposed items must score higher than 12%. Maybe ~18%. This suggests some format learning from the 5% contaminated items.\n\nIn the IRT model:\n- θ_3B_d005 for GSM8K ≈ represents ~18% ability on unexposed items\n- γ captures the boost on exposed items (from 18% to ~60-80%)\n\nFor the counterfactual, if I set exposure to 0, the 3B d005 model would predict around 18% rather than the true clean baseline of 12%, giving a 6 percentage point error. This gap widens for d025 where format learning is even more pronounced, but for d100 where all items are exposed, the counterfactual should approach the true 12% since θ is estimated from cross-model regularization rather than clean item anchors. So θ_d100 can't be identified from the data alone—it relies on the prior pulling it toward the other θ estimates. In the MCMC sampling, the posterior for θ_3B_d100_GSM8K gets constrained by the prior centered near θ_d000, while still needing to explain the observed 70% accuracy. If γ is around 3 in logit space, then the effective ability becomes θ + γ ≈ 1.0, which would produce the right accuracy level through the 3PL model.\n\nThe item parameters are estimated jointly across all models, so the 3B d000 data constrains what the item difficulties should be—items that only 12% of models answer correctly have moderate difficulty. This is getting quite involved, so I'm going to use reasonable median-tier estimates and move forward. Looking at the per-benchmark error metrics for the median tier across contaminated conditions, MMLU shows around 3.8 percentage points of mean absolute error, with the 0.5B regression being the dominant source of error before partial correction.\n\nNow examining the per-benchmark γ values that capture how much contamination shifts performance: MMLU has a small γ of 0.12 with a confidence interval that includes zero, suggesting contamination barely affects performance at the 1.5B and 3B scales. GSM8K shows a much larger γ of 1.8, driven primarily by format recovery in the 3B model, with a confidence interval that excludes zero. ARC has a very small γ of 0.08, also with an interval spanning zero. I'm realizing these γ values